neofetch-elks 1.0

Small native C adaptation of the neofetch system-summary idea for real-mode
ELKS on 8086/8088 PCs. This is not the upstream Bash script and does not
implement its config files, modules or full command-line interface.

Copy neofetch to /bin on an ELKS filesystem, or run it from its current
folder. This package does not contain an installer or write any SD card.

  neofetch --tandy
  neofetch --stdout
  neofetch --no-color
  neofetch --help

--tandy selects a Tandy 1000 EX / 8088 label; it does not detect hardware.
OS and machine come from uname. User comes from the current UID. Shell and
terminal are environment values, not detected processes. ELKS main memory
and uptime use read-only /dev/kmem queries. If unavailable they say so.
Memory excludes reserved BIOS/video/kernel space and is not installed RAM.
Uptime wraps with the 32-bit kernel tick counter (about 497 days at 100 Hz).
No package counts, clock speed, video mode, sound or GPU capabilities are
invented. Color is disabled on redirected output, TERM=dumb or NO_COLOR.
This build targets real mode, not the ELKS 286 protected-mode variant.

Build with an installed ELKS ia16 toolchain and matching ELKS source tree:
  make ELKS_ROOT=/path/to/elks TOOLCHAIN=/path/to/toolchain
The ELKS tree must include built elks/tools/bin/elf2elks.

The native target uses -march=i8086. The optional host target is only a
rendering/CLI check and deliberately does not probe host memory or uptime:
  make host
  python3 test_cli.py

MIT licensed; see LICENSE. No upstream Bash runtime is required.
